Lecture 7 — Revision 1
Goal of this lecture: consolidate everything from Lectures 1–6 into one mental model — an end-to-end RAG application — and build the habit of debugging a failing retrieval pipeline systematically. By the end you should be able to draw the whole architecture from prompt to streaming UI, and reason your way from a wrong answer back to the box that caused it.
This is a revision lecture. There is little new material here. Instead it is a structured review of the first block, a self-test question bank, and a checklist you can run down when you build (or fix) a RAG app for HW3 and your project.

1. The one-picture mental model
Everything in Lectures 1–6 fits into a single application. Split it into two phases:

Offline — build the index once.

Load documents (PDF, Markdown, HTML…).
Chunk them into ~200–500-token passages with a small overlap, and attach metadata (source file, page, section).
Embed each chunk with an embedding model → a vector.
Store the vectors + text + metadata in a vector store (pgvector; Qdrant / Pinecone for scale).
Online — answer a question.

The UI sends the question to your backend (POST /api/chat).
The backend embeds the question with the same embedding model.
It searches the store for the top-k nearest chunks (cosine similarity).
It assembles a prompt: the retrieved chunks as context + the question.
It calls the LLM at temperature=0 and streams the answer (SSE) back to the UI, with citations built from the chunks' metadata.
            OFFLINE                                 ONLINE
docs → chunk+meta → embed → [vector store] ← embed-query ← question (UI → backend)
                                  │
                          top-k chunks → assemble prompt → LLM(temp=0) → SSE → UI
